### Summary

When a single token names the same expert in more than one of its `ids` slots,
the CUDA backend disagrees with the CPU reference, and in some shapes dies
with an illegal memory access. `ggml.h` documents no uniqueness requirement on
`ids`, and the CPU backend handles repeats fine, so the two backends currently
implement different semantics.

`tests/test-backend-ops.cpp` never exercises this: `init_mul_mat_id_tensors`
fills each ids row with `i % n_mats` and shuffles it, so within a row the ids
are distinct by construction.

### Reproducer

The patch below adds a `repeat_ids` mode to `test_mul_mat_id` and registers
cases across f16/q8_0/q4_0, batch 1/16/256, broadcast and non-broadcast `b`.
Mode 1 repeats every pair (`data[i] = (i/2) % n_mats`); mode 2 repeats exactly
one pair per row, so that the size of the error shows how much of the output
is affected.

```sh
git apply repeated-ids-repro.patch
cmake --build build --target test-backend-ops -j
./build/bin/test-backend-ops -o MUL_MAT_ID
```

<details>
<summary>repeated-ids-repro.patch</summary>

```diff
diff --git a/tests/test-backend-ops.cpp b/tests/test-backend-ops.cpp
index 6688deb..88ff1eb 100644
--- a/tests/test-backend-ops.cpp
+++ b/tests/test-backend-ops.cpp
@@ -4404,7 +4404,7 @@ struct test_mul_mat_hadamard : public test_mul_mat {
     }
 };

-static void init_mul_mat_id_tensors(ggml_context * ctx, int n_mats) {
+static void init_mul_mat_id_tensors(ggml_context * ctx, int n_mats, int repeat_ids = 0) {
     std::random_device rd;
     std::default_random_engine rng(rd());
     for (ggml_tensor * t = ggml_get_first_tensor(ctx); t != NULL; t = ggml_get_next_tensor(ctx, t)) {
@@ -4413,10 +4413,30 @@ static void init_mul_mat_id_tensors(ggml_context * ctx, int n_mats) {
             // ids
             for (int64_t r = 0; r < ggml_nrows(t); r++) {
                 std::vector<int32_t> data(t->ne[0]);
-                for (int i = 0; i < t->ne[0]; i++) {
-                    data[i] = i % n_mats;
+                if (repeat_ids == 2) {
+                    // exactly one duplicated pair per row: if only the
+                    // duplicated slots are wrong, the error is a fraction of
+                    // the rows, not all of them
+                    for (int i = 0; i < t->ne[0]; i++) {
+                        data[i] = i % n_mats;
+                    }
+                    if (t->ne[0] > 1) { data[1] = data[0]; }
+                } else if (repeat_ids) {
+                    // The same expert named twice in a row. Nothing forbids
+                    // this: the CPU path handles it, and a caller that
+                    // substitutes ids (padding a partial expert set, for
+                    // instance) will produce it. Left unshuffled so that any
+                    // prefix of the row - which is what a view of n_used
+                    // columns takes - contains a repeat.
+                    for (int i = 0; i < t->ne[0]; i++) {
+                        data[i] = (i / 2) % n_mats;
+                    }
+                } else {
+                    for (int i = 0; i < t->ne[0]; i++) {
+                        data[i] = i % n_mats;
+                    }
+                    std::shuffle(data.begin(), data.end(), rng);
                 }
-                std::shuffle(data.begin(), data.end(), rng);
                 ggml_backend_tensor_set(t, data.data(), r * t->nb[1], t->ne[0] * sizeof(int32_t));
             }
         } else {
@@ -4435,9 +4455,10 @@ struct test_mul_mat_id : public test_case {
     const int64_t m;
     const int64_t n;
     const int64_t k;
+    const int repeat_ids; // 0 none, 1 every pair repeats, 2 one pair repeats

     std::string vars() override {
-        return VARS_TO_STR8(type_a, type_b, n_mats, n_used, b, m, n, k);
+        return VARS_TO_STR9(type_a, type_b, n_mats, n_used, b, m, n, k, repeat_ids);
     }

